  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe long-term period begins on Tuesday, which is the first day of \nastronomical fall! While conditions will be far from autumnal, we \nwill get a break from the heat (at least somewhat) from Wednesday \nonward, as temperatures drop to around normal for this time of year.\n\nSPC has all of north Georgia and parts of central Georgia under a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe storms on Tuesday as a 500-\nmb shortwave trough traverses the Tennessee River Valley. Ample \ninstability should blossom ahead of and along this feature, sparking \nconvection that may develop upscale into a QLCS/MCS (suggested by \nthe RRFS [Rapid Refresh Forecast System]). As a result, the peak in \nthunderstorm coverage across parts of north and central Georgia may \nend up being early Tuesday evening into the overnight hours, as we \noften see with these systems. Damaging wind gusts would be the \nprimary hazard, with small hail, frequent lightning, and heavy \nrainfall as secondary hazards. The degree to which convection \nweakens as it pushes through the forecast area during nocturnal \nstabilization will come into better view as we enter the hi-res \nmodel time window (24-36 hours out).\n\nA strong area of high pressure will anchor itself over eastern \nCanada from midweek through the weekend, leading to cold air damming \n(the \"wedge\") here across the forecast area. While the associated \nairmass will be drier overall, enough moisture will remain in place \ninitially to support isolated to scattered showers and storms on \nWednesday and Thursday. Where thunderstorm coverage is maximized on \nWednesday may depend largely on where the outflow boundary from the \nconvection on Tuesday evening/night ends up. FWIW, a few of the AI-\nNWP-based forecasts and GEFS ML forecasts from NCAR suggest the \npotential for a Marginal Risk type day on Wednesday.\n\nEven drier air is progged to move in on Friday and Saturday as high \npressure increases and northerly/northeasterly low-/mid-level flow \nprevails, so rain chances those days are essentially zero area-\nwide.",
